.TH OTPME-TOOL 1 "2025-12-29" "OTPme 0.3.0a120" "OTPme Manual"
.SH NAME
otpme-tool \- OTPme utility commands
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B otpme-tool
.I command
[\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B otpme-tool
provides various utility commands for the OTPme system including realm join/leave, login/logout, synchronization, cryptographic operations, backup/restore and more.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Realm
.TP
.B join \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIdomain\fR]
Join OTPme realm.
.TP
.B leave \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIdomain\fR]
Leave OTPme realm.
.TP
.B get_realm
Show realm of this host.
.TP
.B get_site
Show site of this host.
.TP
.B get_user_site
Get site of login user.
.SS Session
.TP
.B login \fR[\fB\-\-node\fR \fInode\fR] [\fIusername\fR]
Login to OTPme realm.
.TP
.B logout
Logout from OTPme realm.
.TP
.B whoami
Show currently logged in user.
.TP
.B who
Show all users currently logged in on this host (across TTYs and desktop sessions).
.TP
.B show_sessions
Get otpme-agent login sessions.
.TP
.B get_login_session_id
Get otpme-agent login session ID.
.TP
.B get_login_token
Show token of currently logged in user.
.TP
.B get_login_token_type
Show token type of currently logged in user.
.TP
.B get_login_pass_type
Show token password type used at login.
.TP
.B get_tty
Get TTY for logged in user.
.TP
.B get_display
Get \fBDISPLAY\fR of currently logged in user.
.TP
.B get_sotp \fR[\fB\-\-site\fR \fIsite\fR]
Get a SOTP for logged in user.
.TP
.B get_srp
Get a SRP for logged in user.
.TP
.B get_jwt \fIchallenge\fR
Request JWT from mgmtd.
.TP
.B reneg
Try to renegotiate login session.
.TP
.B reset_reauth
Reset auth_on_action reauth.
.TP
.B do_reauth
Trigger a manual \fBauth_on_action\fR reauth, e.g. from a helper script that wants to force the next protected action to prompt for reauthentication.
.SS Synchronization
.TP
.B sync \fR[\fB\-\-realm\fR \fIrealm\fR] [\fB\-\-site\fR \fIsite\fR] [\fIsync_type\fR]
Tell OTPme daemon to start sync with master node. Sync types: sites, objects, token_data, ssh_authorized_keys, nsscache.
.TP
.B resync \fR[\fB\-\-realm\fR \fIrealm\fR] [\fB\-\-site\fR \fIsite\fR] \fIsync_type\fR
Tell OTPme daemon to start resync the given data type.
.TP
.B sync_status
Get time of last successful sync.
.TP
.B do_sync \fR[\fIoptions\fR] \fIsync_type\fR
Do a manual hostd sync. Sync types: objects, token_counters, used_otps, nsscache, ssh_authorized_keys.
.IP
.B \-\-remove\-orphan\-objects
also removes local objects of a type this host no longer syncs. A normal sync never names those objects, so nothing else takes them away. It runs only after a sync that finished and left no object behind.
.TP
.B force_sync \fIobject_id\fR
Write an object again, whole. The search index, the ACLs and the LDIF data are built from the object itself instead of being updated step by step. Entries that were left over are removed this way. Use it when the index of an object does not match the object.
.IP
This also sets the last modified time of the object, so other nodes fetch it again. Hosts do not, because the data a host gets does not include that time.
.IP
It takes an object ID, so it works for every object. This includes the ones without a
.B touch
command, like a host inventory or a used OTP. You need to be admin.
.SS Cryptographic Operations
.TP
.B sign \fR[\fB\-\-stdin\-pass\fR] \fIfile\fR \fIsign_file\fR
Create signature for given file using users RSA key.
.TP
.B verify \fIsign_file\fR \fIfile\fR
Verify signature for given file using users RSA key.
.TP
.B encrypt \fR[\fIoptions\fR] \fIfile\fR \fIoutfile\fR
Encrypt file using users RSA key (AES encryption).
.TP
.B decrypt \fR[\fB\-\-pass\fR \fIpassword\fR] [\fB\-\-stdin\-pass\fR] \fIfile\fR \fIoutfile\fR
Decrypt file using users RSA key.
.SS OTP and MSCHAP Generation
.TP
.B gen_motp \fIepoch_time\fR \fIsecret\fR \fIpin\fR [\fIotp_count\fR]
Generate mOTP OTPs from epoch time, secret and pin.
.TP
.B gen_mschap \fIusername\fR \fIpassword\fR
Generate MSCHAP challenge/response from given username and password.
.TP
.B gen_refresh \fIusername\fR \fIpassword\fR
Generate SRP (Session-Refresh-Password) from given password.
.TP
.B gen_refresh_mschap \fIusername\fR \fIpassword\fR
Generate SRP challenge/response (MSCHAP) from given username and password.
.TP
.B gen_logout \fIusername\fR \fIpassword\fR
Generate SLP (Session-Logout-Password) from given password.
.TP
.B gen_logout_mschap \fIusername\fR \fIpassword\fR
Generate MSCHAP SLP challenge/response from given username and password.
.SS Signer Management
.TP
.B add_signer \fR[\fB\-\-private\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-pin\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-verify\-token\-opts\fR] [\fB\-\-allow\-throwaway\-key\fR] [\fB\-\-tag\fR \fItag\fR] \fB\-\-signer\-type\fR \fItype\fR \fIobject_id\fR
Add signer. A token signature includes the token options (e.g.
.IR command= )
the SSH key may be used with, and those are verified against the options the key is served with.
.B \-\-no\-verify\-token\-opts
turns that check off for this signer.
.RS
.PP
.B \-\-allow\-throwaway\-key
is only for a
.B remote_access
signer.
It lets that signer ask for a shell on a key the job brings along, instead of on a key this host already holds for the user.
That way in needs nothing but the signature of the job, so it is off unless you say otherwise.
See
.BR otpme\-shell (1).
.RE
.TP
.B del_signer \fR[\fB\-\-private\fR] \fIsigner_uuid\fR
Delete signer.
.TP
.B enable_signer \fR[\fB\-\-private\fR] [\fB\-\-type\fR \fIsigner_type\fR] \fIsigner_uuid\fR
Enable signer.
.TP
.B disable_signer \fR[\fB\-\-private\fR] [\fB\-\-type\fR \fIsigner_type\fR] \fIsigner_uuid\fR
Disable signer.
.TP
.B update_signer \fR[\fB\-\-private\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-pin\fR] [\fB\-\-remove\-gone\fR] [\fB\-\-verify\-token\-opts\fR|\fB\-\-no\-verify\-token\-opts\fR] [\fB\-\-allow\-throwaway\-key\fR|\fB\-\-no\-throwaway\-key\fR] [\fB\-\-signer\-type\fR \fItype\fR] [\fIsigner_uuid\fR]
Update signer.
Pins the current key of the user or role again, for example after a key change.
.RS
.PP
The last two pairs change what the signer may ask for.
Name neither of a pair and it is left as it is: an update is usually about the pinned key, and saying nothing must not take a permission away.
This is the only way to change one of them without removing the signer and adding it again.
.RE
.TP
.B show_signer \fR[\fB\-\-private\fR] [\fB\-\-signer\-type\fR \fItype\fR] [\fIsigner_uuid\fR]
Show signer(s).
The table says whether the signature has to cover the token options, and whether the signer may bring its own key for a remote access session.
.SS Host Keys
.TP
.B update_host_keys \fR[\fB\-\-list\fR] [\fIhost\fR]
Take over the public keys of hosts whose key changed.
Only with \fBPIN_HOST_KEYS\fR, see
.BR otpme.conf (5).
.RS
.PP
With that parameter the public key of every host synced here is written down, and a host is held to it.
A key that differs from the one written down does not replace it, and a remote access session to that host does not open until somebody takes the new key over.
This is where that happens, and nothing else does it.
.PP
It shows every host whose key in the realm is not the one written down here, with both fingerprints, and asks once.
The keys are read from the host objects when it runs, so what it shows is the realm as it is at that moment.
What it shows is what it will take over.
Name a \fIhost\fR to take over that one alone.
.PP
You must be root, because the keys are read from this host's copy of the realm, and the global \fB\-f\fR skips the question.
.B \-\-list
only shows and changes nothing.
.RE
.SS Offline Token
.TP
.B show_offline_token \fR[\fItoken_id\fR]
Show cached offline token(s).
.TP
.B pin_offline_token
Pin cached offline token(s).
.TP
.B unpin_offline_token
Unpin cached offline token(s).
.SS Object Management
.TP
.B dump \fIcache_type\fR [\fIobject_id\fR]
Tell OTPme daemon to dump the given cache.
.TP
.B dump_object \fIobject_id\fR
Dump object.
.TP
.B dump_index \fR[\fIobject_id\fR]
Dump object index.
.TP
.B delete_object \fIobject_id\fR
Delete object.
.TP
.B check_duplicate_ids \fIobject_type\fR
Check for duplicate uidNumber/gidNumber.
.TP
.B search \fIattribute=\fR\fIvalue\fR \fIobject_type=\fR\fItype\fR \fR[\fIreturn_type=\fR\fIuuid|full_oid|read_oid|name\fR]
Search OTPme objects.
.SS Import
.TP
.B import \fR[\fB\-\-password\fR \fIpassword\fR] \fIfile\fR
Import object config.
.TP
.B add_user \fIfile\fR
Create users listed in file.
.TP
.B mass_object_add \fR[\fB\-\-verify\-only\fR] [\fB\-\-procs\fR \fIN\fR] \fIcsv_file\fR
Add objects from CSV file.
.SS Service Management
.TP
.B reload
Tell OTPme to reload its config.
.TP
.B index \fIcommand\fR
Execute index command (start, status, stop, cli, init, drop, rebuild, create_db_indices, drop_db_indices).
.TP
.B cache \fIcommand\fR
Execute cache command.
.TP
.B radius \fIcommand\fR
Execute radius command (start, status, stop, reload, restart, test).
.SS Private Keys in otpme-agent
These three are what the key script uses.
They are listed here because a human may want to look, not because a human has to use them.
.PP
otpme-agent holds the private keys of the login session in two shapes: as the login brought them, wrapped with the key password of the user, and unwrapped, as the key script left them after it was first asked to use one.
The unwrapped one lives for \fBkey_cache_time\fR of the user.
Both are only handed to the login session they belong to.
See \fBenable_key_caching\fR in
.BR otpme\-user (1).
.TP
.B get_private_key \fB\-\-role\fR \fIsign\fR|\fIencrypt\fR \fR[\fB\-\-decrypted\fR]
Write a private key of the logged in user to stdout.
Without \fB\-\-decrypted\fR the wrapped one, with it the unwrapped one.
It fails when the agent holds none, which is the normal answer before the key script has been asked to use the key, and after the cache time ran out.
.TP
.B add_private_key \fB\-\-role\fR \fIsign\fR|\fIencrypt\fR \fR[\fB\-\-decrypted\fR]
Give the agent a private key, read from stdin.
\fB\-\-decrypted\fR for the unwrapped one, which is then kept for the cache time.
.br
From stdin, because a private key on a command line is a private key in the process list.
.TP
.B del_private_key \fB\-\-role\fR \fIsign\fR|\fIencrypt\fR
Take a private key away from the agent, both shapes of it.
The next use asks for the key password again.
This is how to drop a cached key without logging out.
.SS Key and Certificate
.TP
.B regen_master_key
Regen AES master key.
.TP
.B renew_auth_key
Renew host auth key.
.TP
.B renew_cert
Renew host certificate.
.SS SSH Agent
.TP
.B start_ssh_agent
Start users SSH agent script.
.TP
.B stop_ssh_agent
Stop users SSH agent script.
.TP
.B restart_ssh_agent
Restart users SSH agent script.
.TP
.B ssh_agent_status
Get users SSH agent script status.
.SS Backup and Restore
.TP
.B backup \fB\-d\fR \fIbackup_dir\fR \fR[\fB\-\-remove\-older\-than\fR \fItime\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
Write backup to backup directory.
.TP
.B restore \fR{\fB\-d\fR \fIrestore_dir\fR | \fB\-f\fR \fIrestore_file\fR}
Restore from backup.
.SS Benchmark
.TP
.B login_benchmark \fR[\fB\-\-procs\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-node\fR \fInode\fR] \fIcsv_file\fR
Run login benchmark.
.SS Smartcard
.TP
.B detect_smartcard \fR[\fB\-t\fR \fItype1,type2\fR]
Detect connected smartcards.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Join Options
.TP
.B \-\-jotp \fIJOTP\fR
Join using the given JOTP.
.TP
.B \-\-host\-type \fInode|host\fR
Join host as type.
.TP
.B \-\-unit \fIUNIT\fR
Join host to the given unit.
.TP
.B \-\-host\-key\-len \fILENGTH\fR
Host/Node key length.
.TP
.B \-\-site\-key\-len \fILENGTH\fR
Site key length.
.TP
.B \-\-trust\-site\-cert
Trust any site certificate.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-site\-cert \fIFINGERPRINT\fR
Check the site certificate fingerprint.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-daemon\-start
Don't start OTPme daemons after joining realm.
.SS Leave Options
.TP
.B \-\-lotp \fILOTP\fR
Leave using the given LOTP.
.TP
.B \-\-offline
Leave realm without talking to OTPme servers.
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-host
Do not delete node/host object on server side.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-keep\-host
Delete node/host object on server side.
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-data
Keep all data (realm data, certs, offline tokens).
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-cache
Keep cached data (offline tokens, nsscache etc.).
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-cert
Do not revoke host certificate when leaving.
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-auth\-key
Do not revoke host auth key when leaving.
.SS Encrypt Options
.TP
.B \-\-rsa
Encrypt file using RSA encryption.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-rsa
Disable use of RSA public keys for encryption of AES keys.
.TP
.B \-u \fIUSERNAME\fR
Encrypt file with public key of given user.
.TP
.B \-\-pass \fIPASSWORD\fR
Use password to encrypt/decrypt the file (AES only).
.TP
.B \-\-stdin\-pass
Read password/passphrase from stdin.
.TP
.B \-\-force\-pass
Force encryption with password (AES only).
.SS Do_sync Options
.TP
.B \-\-realm \fIREALM\fR
Realm to sync.
.TP
.B \-\-site \fISITE\fR
Site to sync.
.TP
.B \-\-resync
Do a complete resync.
.TP
.B \-\-offline
Sync offline token data.
.TP
.B \-\-sync\-older\-objects
Sync objects even if they are older than the local ones.
.TP
.B \-\-ignore\-changed\-objects
Sync objects even if they changed while syncing.
.SS Signer Options
.TP
.B \-\-private
Operate on a signer of the logged in user instead of a system-wide signer. Without this option, signer commands operate on the host's signer configuration (root only).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-pin
Do not pin signature keys. By default signer keys are pinned, which means only the exact key present at the time the signer was added is trusted. With \fB\-\-no\-pin\fR, any valid key of the signer object is accepted.
.TP
.B \-\-signer\-type \fITYPE\fR
What this signer is trusted for. Supported types: \fBtoken\fR, \fBkey_script\fR, \fBpush_script\fR, \fBauth_script\fR, \fBlogin_script\fR, \fBagent_script\fR.
.br
Types for configuration jobs (see
.BR otpme\-deploy (1)):
\fBsw_mgmt\fR, \fBrepo_mgmt\fR, \fBsigner_mgmt\fR, \fBservice_mgmt\fR, \fBfile_mgmt\fR, \fBhost_mgmt\fR.
A host only runs a job if it is signed by a signer of the job type.
\fBfile_mgmt\fR, \fBsigner_mgmt\fR and \fBhost_mgmt\fR give full control over the host.
.TP
.B \-\-tag \fITAG\fR
Add tag to signer (can be used multiple times).
.PP
Global options are available for all commands. See
.BR otpme (1)
for details.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B otpme-tool join \-\-jotp abc123 example.com
Join OTPme realm
.TP
.B otpme-tool login alice
Login as user alice
.TP
.B otpme-tool sync objects
Start object sync
.TP
.B otpme-tool backup \-d /backup/otpme
Create backup
.TP
.B otpme-tool detect_smartcard
Detect connected smartcards
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/otpme/
OTPme data directory
